      <h1>Heat and Lead-Acid Batteries</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Heat, not cold, is the thing that <em>kills</em> lead-acid batteries. Cold makes them start hard; heat quietly
        ages them fast — by accelerating the chemical reactions that corrode, dry out and discharge the battery.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Quick answer:</strong> Higher temperature speeds up every ageing reaction in a lead-acid battery —
        grid corrosion, water loss and self-discharge. A widely used rule of thumb is that battery life roughly
        <strong>halves for every ~8–10 °C of sustained temperature above ~25 °C</strong>. This is why the engine bay,
        not the winter, is where batteries actually age.
      </div>

      <h2>What causes heat degradation?</h2>
      <p>
        The cause is sustained elevated temperature — most often the engine compartment in summer, but also a charger
        that holds the battery too hot, or a battery installed without heat shielding.
      </p>

      <h2>How does heat degrade a battery?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Grid corrosion</strong> — the positive grid oxidises faster as temperature rises.</li>
        <li><strong>Water loss</strong> — gassing and evaporation accelerate, drying out sealed batteries.</li>
        <li><strong>Self-discharge</strong> — roughly doubles with each ~10 °C rise, so a hot battery goes flat faster in storage.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The temperature dependence is exponential (Arrhenius behaviour): reaction rates roughly double per 10 °C, so a
        battery that lives 6 years at 25 °C may last only ~3 years at 35 °C — the origin of the halving rule of thumb.
      </p>

      <h2>What are the symptoms of heat degradation?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Shortened service life with no other obvious fault.</li>
        <li>Corroded or swollen case, low electrolyte (flooded), dry-out (sealed).</li>
        <li>Faster self-discharge when parked.</li>
        <li>Weak cranking despite normal charging.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How is the heat effect measured?</h2>
      <p>
        There is no single "temperature gauge" — the practical signals are electrolyte specific gravity (flooded),
        <a href="/technology/internal-resistance/">internal resistance</a>/conductance, and water usage. The key is correlating them with the battery's operating
        temperature. Chargers with temperature compensation measure battery temperature and adjust voltage down when
        hot, which is the direct prevention tool.
      </p>

      <h2>How is heat degradation diagnosed?</h2>
      <p>
        If a battery fails early with signs of corrosion and water loss but normal charging habits, sustained heat is
        the usual suspect. A hot-running battery that gasses or bulges while charging points to both heat <em>and</em>
        an over-voltage charger — see <a href="/lead-acid/charging-problems/">charging problems</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>How do you prevent heat degradation?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Use a charger with temperature compensation (lowers voltage when hot).</li>
        <li>Add heat shielding or relocate the battery where possible.</li>
        <li>Ventilate the bay and keep the battery clean (dirt holds heat).</li>
        <li>Store batteries cool and charged.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What can these methods not tell you?</h2>
      <p>
        The "halves per 8–10 °C" figure is a <strong>rule of thumb</strong>, not a precise law — actual life depends on
        battery construction, duty cycle and charging. But the direction is unambiguous: sustained heat always shortens
        life, and the damage is largely irreversible once corrosion and dry-out occur.
      </p>
      <h2>The doubling rule, quantified</h2>
      <p>
        The temperature law that governs every heat claim: battery aging reactions roughly <strong>double their
        rate per 10°C</strong> of temperature rise. A battery aging at 25°C ages about twice as fast at 35°C and
        about four times as fast at 45°C — the arithmetic behind the rule that heat is the battery's most
        expensive environment (see <a href="/lead-acid/self-discharge/">self-discharge</a> for the same law in
        storage).
      </p>


      <h2>World Battery Hub Analysis</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>My take:</strong> The most misunderstood fact about lead-acid batteries is that cold is annoying but
          heat is fatal — people replace a battery that "died in winter" when it was actually aged all summer in the
          engine bay.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The reasoning:</strong> The exponential temperature dependence means a few degrees of sustained heat
          matter more than a cold snap. Understanding that reframes the whole "why did my battery die" question from
          weather to operating temperature.
        </p>
        <p class="muted"><em>My editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

      <h2>What buyers should ask</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Will the battery live in a hot engine bay or climate where life roughly halves each 10°C above 25°C?</li>
        <li>Is the battery's placement or ventilation addressable to reduce heat exposure?</li>
        <li>Does the hot-climate application justify a heat-tolerant or <a href="/lead-acid/agm-battery/">AGM</a> design?</li>
        <li>Is the shortened life being priced in, rather than expecting the full rated lifespan in heat?</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>The bottom line</h2>
      <p>Heat is lead-acid's quiet killer — roughly a halving of life for every 10°C above 25°C, from accelerated corrosion and water loss. In hot climates, plan for a shorter replacement window and reduce heat exposure where the mounting allows.</p>

                  <h2>An original calculation: the halving rule</h2>
      <p>
        Corrosion-limited battery life roughly halves with every 10°C above about 25°C. A battery engineered
        for 4 years at 25°C ambient runs on the order of 2 years at 35°C and 1 year at 45°C. The rule runs
        backward too: every degree of cooler mounting is free battery life — which is why battery location
        and ventilation are procurement decisions, not installation details.
      </p>

      <h2>Engineering interpretation</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p><strong>Where I stand:</strong> Heat is the one battery-life variable the buyer controls at zero parts cost — a cooler mounting location outperforms a more expensive chemistry on cost-per-year.</p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> The halving rule is relentless and free to exploit in both directions. Ventilation, heat shields and location choice are the cheapest life-extension engineering that exists in automotive batteries.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>Engineering interpretation, not purchasing advice.</em></p>
      </div>
